  "anchor": "STYLE ANCHOR - changjak-a04  (duck / Dutch canal / walking backwards)\n\nStyle: a hand-drawn picture-book page for 4-6 year olds. Quiet, cool, observant.\n  Almost wordless - the drawing has to carry the whole story, so it must be legible before it is pretty.\n\nMEDIUM: soft graphite pencil and diluted ink wash on cool grey-white paper with a fine tooth.\n  Every mark is PRESSED into the paper: the graphite is dark and waxy where it is pushed hard,\n  and skips over the tooth where it is dragged light. Edges are made by the pencil running out,\n  never by a clean vector line. Water and air are one flat pass of grey ink wash, laid once and\n  left alone, with the brush edge still visible where it dried.\n  One single thin colour plate of warm amber is laid over the finished drawing, like a second\n  print run - it sits ON TOP of the graphite and does not blend with it.\n  No blending, no soft airbrush gradient, no digital glow.\n\nPALETTE: a cold blue-leaning graphite monochrome covers the entire book.\n  Hex anchors: paper #E9EAE7 / mid graphite #6E7A80 / deep graphite #2E3438 /\n  pressed-hard darkest (footprints, feet, wet brick joints) #1F2427.\n  EXACTLY ONE other colour exists in this book: warm amber #E5A23C, deepening to #C9762A.\n  🔴 AMBER IS LAMPLIGHT AND BEING-SEEN, AND ITS AMOUNT IS THE STORY. It appears ONLY where\n  a per-page instruction says so. On every other page there is no amber anywhere at all.\n  Nothing else is ever amber - not the duck, not its bill, not the bricks, not the tulips,\n  not the sun. Brick, roof tile, tulip and sunset are all rendered in graphite only.\n\nCOMPOSITION: low-information field first, subject second.\n  The duck is small in frame (about 1/7 of page height) except p2, p5, p9 and p10.\n  Canal, quay and rooflines are horizontal bands; the eye is led by diagonals, never by a\n  centred symmetrical subject - except p6 and p10, which are deliberate mirror compositions.\n  🔴 Keep the bottom 18% of the image quiet and free of key subject matter (a caption band is\n  laid over it later). Where the ground fills the frame, keep the bottom strip plain wet brick.\n\nFINISH HIERARCHY - read this twice. This is about how FINISHED each area is, NOT about opacity.\n  1. THE DUCK AND THE FOOTPRINTS = finished. Line and tone, worked, pressed dark.\n  2. WHAT THE DUCK TOUCHES OR LOOKS AT on that page (the one bridge beam, the one chimney,\n     the one shard of reflected village) = half-finished: contour plus one pass of tone.\n  3. EVERYTHING ELSE = A LOOSE UNFINISHED PENCIL SKETCH. A few open contour lines and direction\n     strokes, barely any tone, bare paper showing through, corners of shapes left unclosed,\n     deliberately not filled in - like the rough underdrawing on a sketchbook page.\n  🔴 The background is NOT faded, NOT hazy, NOT blurred and NOT desaturated. It is simply not\n  drawn to completion. A rough dark pencil line is correct; a soft pale finished line is wrong.\n  Never draw every roof tile, every brick, every window pane, every tulip petal.\n  EXCEPTION - exactly two pages carry density: the page of the reflected village and the\n  wordless page of the backs of the houses. On those two, level 2 finish extends to the props.\n\nCHARACTER DESIGN: eyes are DRAWN, not dotted - a small dark almond with a SEPARATE drawn\n  eyebrow line above it, so the face can sulk, be startled, and be quietly content.\n  Bodies read as one soft mass plus a neck and a tail. Bills are two or three pencil strokes.\n  The silhouette must be readable at thumbnail size.\n\n  FACE SEPARATION (required): the face must read apart from the body in VALUE, not by adding\n  a colour - a lighter muzzle or brow patch, bare paper, or a tonal step at the jaw. Eyes,\n  brow and mouth must never sink into one flat body mass. Test: at thumbnail size the\n  expression is still legible. This does NOT add a colour to the palette above.\nSETTING: a Dutch canal town - narrow tall stepped-gable brick houses shoulder to shoulder along\n  the water, tulip pots on window ledges, bicycles laid on their sides, mooring rope and\n  bollards, a white wooden drawbridge, a windmill at the end of the road, clogs by a door.\n  European, no Asian architectural motifs.\n\nCANVAS: 16:9 double-page spread, 4-6 year old picture book.\n\nNOT: NOT digital airbrush / NOT smooth gradients / NOT glossy 3D CG render / NOT cel-shaded\n  anime / NOT a texture filter laid over flat digital colour / NOT photographic /\n  NOT a fully rendered background / NOT every roof tile, brick, leaf or window drawn to\n  completion / NOT a uniform finish across the page / NOT a hazy, blurry or faded background\n  (that is blur, not un-drawn) / NOT a second accent colour / NOT amber anywhere except where\n  the page instruction places it / NOT any lettering, numerals, shop signage or house numbers\n  anywhere in the image / NOT wool felt, NOT stitched fabric, NOT sculpted clay (another line\n  owns those).",
