   "CHARACTER SHEET - DuckBack   (bake this FIRST, before any scene)\n\n🔴 THE SHEET IS DRAWN IN THE SAME MEDIUM AS THE BOOK. Soft graphite pencil and diluted ink wash\n  on cool grey-white toothed paper #E9EAE7. The pencil grain must be visible on the body, the\n  contour must be left open in places, and the graphite must be pressed dark on the feet.\n  Do NOT render this character smoothly just because there is no background behind it.\n  There is NO amber anywhere on this sheet.\n\nFACE: a long flat bill, the tip turned very slightly DOWN, so a small sulk is built into the\n  anatomy and survives every mood - this is the single most important design decision.\n  Eye = a small dark almond #1F2427 with a SEPARATE drawn eyebrow stroke above it, set low and\n  close to the bill. No dot eye, no blush, no highlight dot, no glossy catchlight.\nFEATHERS: body a soft mid graphite grey #6E7A80 with the paper showing through on the breast;\n  wing coverts a darker pressed grey #2E3438 in three or four short strokes, not feather by\n  feather; throat and cheek left as bare paper.\nBILL & FEET: bill in mid graphite. 🔴 FEET ARE THE PLOT - webbed feet drawn deliberately large\n  for the body, pressed the darkest on the whole character #1F2427, with the three toes clearly\n  separated so a footprint can be recognised as this duck's foot.\nBUILD & SILHOUETTE: a fat teardrop body about two and a half heads tall, carried LOW and level\n  on a long neck that it holds forward and down rather than up - most storybook ducks hold the\n  neck high, this one does not, because it is always looking at the ground and behind itself.\n  Legs set well back so it leans.\nSIGNATURE DETAIL: 🔴 ONE crooked tail feather that sticks out the opposite way from all the\n  others. It is the whole book in one feather, and it is how the reader tells this duck from\n  the five others. Keep it in every single drawing, including the back views.\nTHE OTHER FIVE DUCKS: identical species and silhouette, ALWAYS seen from behind, never given a\n  face, never given the crooked feather. They exist only as a row of same-shaped backs.\nREFERENCE SHEET: full-body side view standing / three-quarter turn stepping BACKWARDS with one\n  webbed foot reaching behind / back view showing the crooked tail feather / a large detail of\n  one webbed foot and, next to it, the wet print that foot leaves /\n  three expression close-ups: sulking (brow low and level, eye half lidded, bill tipped down),\n  startled (brow high, eye wide and round, bill open a crack),\n  quietly pleased (eye a downward crescent, brow relaxed, neck loose).\n  Plain paper background, no scenery, no amber.\nFACE SEPARATION (required): on this sheet the face must read apart from the body in VALUE,\n  not by adding a colour - a lighter muzzle/cheek patch, bare paper, or a tonal step at the jaw.\n  Eyes, brow and mouth must never sink into one flat body mass. Test: shrink this sheet to\n  thumbnail size - the expression must still be legible. This does NOT add a colour.\nSCENE token: DuckBack."
